ative speed. When the dog turns around at home after the first meeting, it starts moving towards Feifei. By the time the dog reaches home, Feifei has walked an additional (time taken for dog to return home) * v_f. Then, the dog catches up to Feifei, who is now at some distance. Let\'s formalize.\n\nLet me define t2 as the time from when the dog turns around (after returning from first meeting) until the second meeting. In this time, the dog travels towards Feifei at speed v_d, and Feifei travels away from the first meeting point (200 meters) at speed v_f. The relative speed is v_d - v_f. The distance between them when the dog turns around is the distance between Feifei\'s position when the dog returns and the second meeting point.\n\nWait, when the dog returns to home, Feifei is at 200 + t2\' * v_f, where t2\' is the time since the first meeting until the dog returns. Wait, this seems tangled.\n\nAlternatively, let’s think in terms of when the second meeting occurs.\n\nTotal duration from departure until second meeting:\n\n- 3 minutes (head start), plus time dog took to reach home after first meeting (200 / v_d), plus the time Feifei walked during that 200 / v_d minutes (which is (200 / v_d) * v_f), so Feifei is at 200 + (200 / v_d) * v_f when the dog arrives home. Then, the dog turns around and starts moving towards Feifei. The distance between them is 200 + (200 / v_d) * v_f. Then, they meet after time t3, moving towards each other at speed v_d - v_f. So:\n\nDistance between them when dog turns around: 200 + (200 / v_d) * v_f = 200 + 200 * (v_f / v_d)\n\nTime to close this gap: t3 = [200 + 200 * (v_f / v_d)] / (v_d - v_f)\n\nThen, total time from departure: 3 + 200 / v_d + t3\n\nDuring this total time, Feifei has been walking:\n\nDistance from home: 3v_f + t1 v_f + t2\' v_f + t3 v_f = 200 + 200 * (v_f / v_d) + t3 v_f\n\nBut wait, t1 is (200 - 3v_f)/v_f, so t1 = 200 / v_f - 3\n\nSimilarly, t2\' is the time the dog took to return home, which is 200 / v_d. So total time for Feifei is 3 + 200 / v_f (first part) + 200 / v_d (dog\'s return time). Wait, not sure.\n\nAlternatively, Feifei\'s total distance walked is 400 meters. So:\n\n3v_f + t1 v_f + t2 v_f + t3 v_f = 400\n\nWhere t2 is the time from first meeting to dog turning around (i.e., t2 = 200 / v_d), and t3 is the time from dog starting back to second meeting. Wait, no, t2 in this case would need to be clarified.\n\nAlternatively, there might be a better approach using ratios.\n\nSince the first meeting is at 200 meters and the second at 400 meters, perhaps the distances follow a ratio related to their speeds.\n\nAlternatively, think about the time intervals.\n\nWhen the dog first catches up at 200m, then goes back to home, and then catches up again at 400m. The key idea is that in the time between the first and second meetings, the dog travels from the first meeting point to home and back, while Feifei moves forward.\n\nLet me consider the distance the dog travels versus the distance Feifei travels during that interval.\n\nFrom first meeting to second meeting: let\'s call this time interval T.\n\nIn time T, the dog travels to home and then back to Feifei. The dog\'s speed is v_d. So, the distance the dog travels is v_d * T. This must be equal to the distance from the first meeting point (200m) to home (D - 200m) and then back to Feifei\'s new position. So:\n\nv_d * T = 2*(D - 200) + (distance Feifei walked in T time)\n\nBut Feifei, during T time, walks v_f * T meters. So:\n\nv_d * T = 2*(D - 200) + v_f * T\n\nBut D = 3v_d (from first meeting). Therefore:\n\nv_d * T = 2*(3v_d - 200) + v_f * T\n\nSimplify:\n\nv_d T - v_f T = 6v_d - 400\n\nT(v_d - v_f) = 6v_d - 400\n\nSo,\n\nT = (6v_d - 400)/(v_d - v_f)\n\nBut we also know that during T, Feifei walks v_f * T meters, and the second meeting is at 400 meters, so:\n\nTotal distance Feifei has walked when second meeting: 3v_f + T v_f = 400\n\nTherefore:\n\n3v_f + T v_f = 400\n\nFrom above, T = 400 / v_f - 3\n\nSubstituting T into 