n(3 + (200 - 3v_f)/v_f) * v_d = D\n\nMultiply numerator and denominator:\n\n( (3v_f + 200 - 3v_f ) / v_f ) * v_d = D => (200 / v_f ) * v_d = D => 200 * v_d = 200 * v_f => v_d = v_f, which is impossible. So that approach is flawed.\n\nWait, perhaps D is also equal to the distance the dog travels, which is 3v_d. At the same time, D is also equal to (3 + t1) * v_d. But substituting t1, we have:\n\nD = (3 + (200 - 3v_f)/v_f ) * v_d\n\nMultiply out:\n\nD = [ (3v_f + 200 - 3v_f ) / v_f ] * v_d = (200 / v_f) * v_d = 200 * (v_d / v_f )\n\nBut D = 3v_d. Therefore,\n\n3v_d = 200 * (v_d / v_f )\n\nCancel v_d (assuming v_d ≠ 0):\n\n3 = 200 * (v_d / v_f )\n\nWait, but that would imply 3 = 200 (v_d / v_f ), so v_d / v_f = 3/200. But this seems unrelated to the second meeting.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we need to use both meeting points to create a system of equations. Let\'s try again.\n\nFirst meeting:\n\n1) 3v_f + t1 v_f = 200 => t1 = (200 - 3v_f)/v_f\n\nSecond meeting:\n\n- From when the dog turns around until the second meeting, the dog travels back to home and then to Feifei. Let’s denote this time as t2.\n\n- In time t2, the dog travels back to home: 200 meters. Then, turns around and travels towards Feifei. The remaining distance is D - 200 meters. But during this time, Feifei has walked an additional t2 * v_f meters.\n\nWait, when the dog starts back towards home, Feifei is at position 200 meters + t2 * v_f (since the dog took 200 / v_d minutes to return home, so Feifei walked (200 / v_d) * v_f meters during that time). Therefore, when the dog arrives home, Feifei is at 200 + (200 * v_f)/v_d. Then, the dog catches up to Feifei in time t3, moving towards Feifei at speed v_d - v_f. The distance between them when the dog starts back is 200 + (200 * v_f)/v_d. Therefore:\n\nt3 = [200 + (200 * v_f)/v_d] / (v_d - v_f) )\n\nTotal time from departure to second meeting:\n\n3 (dog\'s start) + t1 (time to first meeting) + t2 (time back to home) + t3 (time to second meeting)\n\nBut t2 is 200 / v_d. So total time:\n\n3 + t1 + 200 / v_d + t3\n\nThis is equal to Feifei\'s walking time, which is 400 / v_f.\n\nSo,\n\n400 / v_f = 3 + t1 + 200 / v_d + t3\n\nBut t3 = [200 + (200 v_f / v_d)] / (v_d - v_f). Let\'s substitute:\n\n400 / v_f = 3 + t1 + 200 / v_d + [200 + (200 v_f / v_d)] / (v_d - v_f)\n\nThis seems complex. However, we can substitute t1 from the first meeting:\n\nt1 = (200 - 3v_f)/v_f\n\nSo,\n\n400 / v_f = 3 + (200 - 3v_f)/v_f + 200 / v_d + [200 + (200 v_f / v_d)] / (v_d - v_f)\n\nThis equation can be used to solve for the variables. However, with multiple variables, it\'s difficult. Perhaps we can express v_d in terms of v_f or vice versa.\n\nLet\'s note that D (distance to school) can be expressed in two ways:\n\nFrom first meeting, D = 3v_d. From the second meeting, since total distance Feifei walked is 400 meters, her time is 400 / v_f = 3 + t1 + 200 / v_d as above.\n\nBut D is also the distance the dog covered: 3v_d. Meanwhile, from the second meeting, the dog\'s total time is 3 + t1 + 200 / v_d + t3, and this must equal 400 / v_f.\n\nMaybe there\'s an equation here:\n\nD = 3v_d = 400 / v_f * v_d\n\nTherefore,\n\n3v_d = 400 v_d / v_f\n\nCancel v_d (assuming v_d ≠ 0):\n\n3 = 400 / v_f => v_f = 400 / 3 ≈ 133.333... m/min\n\nThen, v_d = D / 3. From first meeting, D = 200 + 3v_d, so:\n\n200 + 3v_d = 3v_d, which can\'t be. Wait, this is a problem. So 3v_d = D, but from first meeting, D = 200 + 3v_d => 3v_d = 200 + 3v_d => 200 = 0. Contradiction again. So seems there is a fundamental mistake in approach.\n\nAlternative approach:\n\nLet’s let S be the distance from home to school.\n\nFrom the first meeting, when the dog catches up with Feifei 200 meters from home:\n\nFeifei\'s total distance: 3v_f + t1 v_f = 200 => t1 = (200 - 3v_f)/v_f\n\nThe dog\'s total distance: 3v_d + t1 v_d = S => since the dog catches up at S.\n\nThus, S = 3v_d\n\nFrom Feifei\'s side: total time walking 200 meters: 200 / v_f = 3 + t1 = 3 + (200 - 3v_f)/v_f\n\nSimilarly, at the second meeting, 400 meters from home:\n\nFeifei\'s total distance: 3v_f + t1 v_f + t2 v_f = 400 => t2 = (400 - (3v_f + t1 v_f))/v_f = (400 - 200)/v_f = 200 / v_f (since 3v_f + t1 v_f = 200)\n\nBut now Feifei has walked an additional 200 meters after the dog turned around. So, when the dog turns around, Feifei is 400 meters from home. Wait, no. If Feifei has walked 400 meters, she is at school, but the problem states she arrives at 8: ? We need to calculate when.\n\nAlternatively, the key is that after the first meeting, the dog goes back to home, then turns around and meets Feifei again at 400 meters from home. The third meeting is at school.\n\nLet’s model the time.\n\nTotal time until third meeting (arrival at school) for Feifei: 400 / v_f minutes. Which equals 3 minutes (head start) + time dog took to first meet + time dog took to return home + time dog took to second meet.\n\nBut wait, Feifei\'s total walking time is 400 / v_f.\n\nFrom the first meeting to the second meeting: during the time the dog was returning to home, Feifei walks 200 / v_d * v_f meters. Then, from home, she is walked for t3. So total distance walked by Feifei is 200 + (200 / v_d) * v_f + t3 * v_f = 400. So:\n\n200 + 200 (v_f / v_d) + t3 v_f = 400\n\nThen, the dog’s time after first meeting to return and meet again is:\n\nt3 = (S - 200) / (v_d - v_f) = (S - 200) / (v_d - v_f). But S = 3v_d. Thus,\n\nt3 = (3v_d - 200)/(v_d - v_f)\n\nFor Feifei:\n\n400 / v_f = 3 + t1 + (200 / v_d) + t3\n\nWe can substitute t1 = (200 - 3v_f)/v_f\n\nSo:\n\n400 / v_f = 3 + (200 - 3v_f)/v_f + 200 / v_d + (3v_d - 200)/(v_d - v_f)\n\nThis seems complicated, but we can use S = 3v_d and express all in terms of S and variables.\n\nBut perhaps using S and solving:\n\nLet me denote S = 3v_d. So v_d = S / 3.\n\nFrom first meeting:\n\nt1 = (200 - 3v_f)/v_f\n\nFrom second meeting:\n\nt3 = (3v_d - 200)/(v_d - v_f) = (3*(S/3) - 200)/( (S/3) - v_f ) = (S - 200)/( (S/3) - v_f )\n\nFeifei\'s total time:\n\n400 / v_f = 3 + 